<h2>06What actually drives the price</h2>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Driver</th><th>Why it moves the price</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>NC vs. PU paint</td><td>PU costs noticeably more per sq.ft than NC but lasts longer and resists yellowing and moisture better — the single biggest price-and-quality lever in any Duco quote.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Number of coats</td><td>A genuine multi-coat job (primer + 2–3 colour coats + clear top coat) costs more than a thin 1–2 coat "Duco" job, and is the difference most homeowners actually feel after a year.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Gloss level &amp; buffing</td><td>High-gloss finishes need extra sanding/buffing passes to remove texture and achieve true shine, adding labour cost over a flat matte coat.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Colour &amp; effects</td><td>Standard solid colours are cheapest; metallics, pearls, and custom shade-card matches typically cost more in both material and spray time.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>On-site vs. factory spraying</td><td>Factory-sprayed panels (done in a controlled booth) usually finish more evenly than on-site spraying in a half-built home, though on-site is sometimes the only option for fitted items like doors.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Substrate prep &amp; carpentry quality</td><td>Poor MDF quality or sloppy joinery needs extra putty and sanding before paint goes on — and no amount of good paint fixes bad substrate prep underneath it.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Ask before you sign</span>Get the paint brand and whether it's NC or PU written on the quote, along with the number of coats. "Duco finish — ₹/sq.ft" with nothing else specified is the most common way buyers end up underwhelmed.</div>
<h2>07Maintenance and everyday care</h2>
<h3>Do</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Wipe with a soft, dry or barely damp microfibre cloth for daily dusting.</li>
  <li>Use a mild soap-water solution for fingerprints or smudges, especially on high-gloss shutters.</li>
  <li>Keep direct, prolonged sun exposure off Duco-finished doors and panels where possible — UV is the main driver of long-term yellowing, especially on NC paint.</li>
  <li>Address any chipped edge or exposed substrate corner promptly, since that's the entry point for moisture damage underneath the paint film.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Don't</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Don't use abrasive scrubbers, scouring powders, or strong solvents (thinner, acetone) for cleaning — they can dull or strip the top coat.</li>
  <li>Don't let standing water sit at the base of Duco-finished kitchen shutters or near sink cabinets — substrate swelling, not paint failure, is usually what happens first.</li>
  <li>Don't use adhesive hooks or tapes long-term on glossy Duco surfaces; residue removal can mar the sheen.</li>
</ul>
<h2>08Durability and where it actually fails</h2>
<p>Based on common issues reported by carpenters and homeowners over a few years of use, Duco finish problems tend to trace back to one of these:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>NC paint ageing</strong> — nitrocellulose lacquer is more prone to yellowing, surface micro-cracking, and reduced gloss retention over 3–5 years, particularly with sun exposure, compared with PU.</li>
  <li><strong>Inadequate cure time</strong> — panels put into daily use too soon after spraying are more prone to soft spots, fingerprints embedding into the film, and uneven sheen later on.</li>
  <li><strong>Edge and corner chipping</strong> — repeated impact at handle cut-outs, hinges, and panel corners is the most common physical damage point, since paint film is thinner at edges.</li>
  <li><strong>Substrate moisture ingress</strong> — once water gets behind the paint film at an unsealed joint or cut edge, the MDF beneath can swell, lifting or cracking the finish from underneath rather than the paint simply wearing out.</li>
</ul>
<p>A well-specified PU Duco job, properly cured and on good-quality MDF or wood, realistically holds up for 8–15 years of normal household use before a refresh is worth considering; NC-based jobs are more commonly refreshed in the 4–7 year range.</p>
<h2>09Buying checklist — what to confirm before you order</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Confirmed in writing whether the paint system is NC or PU based.</li>
  <li>Number of coats specified (primer, colour coats, clear top coat) — not just "Duco finish" as a line item.</li>
  <li>Gloss level agreed and a physical sample panel inspected in your own lighting, not just a small chip card.</li>
  <li>Substrate confirmed (MDF grade, plywood, or solid wood) and its moisture/water-resistance rating, especially for kitchens and bathrooms.</li>
  <li>Spray environment checked — factory-sprayed panels vs. on-site spraying, and what dust-control measures apply if it's done on-site.</li>
  <li>Cure time before handover clearly stated, so furniture isn't loaded or closed up while still soft.</li>
  <li>Warranty terms covering chipping, yellowing, and peeling — not just general "manufacturing defects" language.</li>
  <li>A maintenance sheet from the vendor specifying safe cleaning agents for that particular paint system.</li>
</ul>
